00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:05,450
Let's start with the most important one, tenses.

18
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:09,530
With maps, the tense relies entirely on the dates provided.

19
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:15,610
Most map questions show you a past date, like 1986, and the present day.

20
00:01:16,230 --> 00:01:19,490
This creates three distinct time zones you must master.

21
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:22,230
Zone one is the past.

22
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:27,970
When you describe what existed in 1986, use the past simple.

23
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:30,990
In 1986, the town was small.

24
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:32,610
There were two farms.

25
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:35,650
Zone two, the present.

26
00:01:36,570 --> 00:01:40,190
When describing what exists right now, use the present simple.

27
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:43,250
Currently, there is a large retirement home.

28
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:45,770
Zone three, the change.

29
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:48,350
This is where students make mistakes.

30
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:52,830
When you describe the transformation that happened between then and now, use

31
00:01:52,830 --> 00:01:54,270
the present perfect passive.

32
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:55,390
Why?

33
00:01:55,890 --> 00:01:59,010
Because the action happened in the past, but we are looking at

34
00:01:59,010 --> 00:01:59,930
the result now.

35
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:03,070
The farms have been replaced by housing.

36
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:05,330
The school has been expanded.

37
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:10,330
Never say, the school was expanded, if you are talking about the

38
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:11,610
result visible today.

39
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:15,190
Use has been expanded instead.

40
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:23,330
Next, let's talk about prepositions.

41
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:26,570
In map questions, you are essentially a tour guide.

42
00:02:27,010 --> 00:02:29,910
If your directions are wrong, the examiner gets lost.

43
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:35,790
The biggest confusion is usually with compass directions, north, south, east, west.

44
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:38,930
There is a specific rule for in versus to.

45
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:41,930
Rule one, in means inside.

46
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:44,890
If a feature is part of the town, use in.

47
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:48,190
The primary school is located in the north of the town.

48
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:49,970
It is inside the borders.

49
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:54,450
Rule two, to means outside or next to.

50
00:02:55,010 --> 00:02:57,630
If you are comparing two things, use to.

51
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:02,050
The golf course is to the north of the town, or the

52
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,170
post office is to the west of the school.

53
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:08,410
Another common error is on versus at for corners.

54
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:12,430
We say a building is on the corner of a street, or

55
00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:13,570
at the junction.

56
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:17,370
The bank is on the corner of High Street and Main Road.

57
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:27,460
Finally, let's tackle the tiny killer that lowers many students' scores.

58
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,440
Articles in map questions, we use the a lot more than usual.

59
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:33,180
Why?

60
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,440
Because the map makes things specific.

61
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:37,440
There isn't just any river.

62
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,840
There is only one river on the map.

63
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:42,380
The golden rule for maps.

64
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,700
If a feature is unique or physical, use the.

65
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:49,940
The river, the bridge, the main road, the railway line.

66
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:52,920
Also, always use the with compass points.

67
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,240
The north, the south, the east, the west.

68
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,140
When do we not use the?

69
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:02,500
We usually do not use the for proper names of towns or

70
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:06,580
singular locations named on the map, unless it is part of the name.

71
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:11,600
Stokeford versus the Stokeford to build a new school.

72
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:17,000
So the pattern is usually introduce a feature with a like a

73
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,040
new school was built.

74
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:22,540
Refer back to it with the like the school is located north of the bank.

75
